{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790175579,"nonce":"o1FIAPHW2obG7x0oIEwqn2CNxis=","title":"The complete window event schemas expose two existing Wren adapter limits:","message":"1. Published Caribou enums expose constructor/index/field getters, but no generated Wren constructor methods. A Wren callback cannot construct an arbitrary ScaleSize or other enum by importing the enum class. The window plugin now supplies physical_scale_size(width,height) and default_scale_size() factories, tested through the same synchronous callback path. General enum construction remains open.\n2. proto::to_wren converts boxed Int64 to f64, so integers outside the exact double range can lose bits. In particular, arbitrary 64-bit touch IDs are exact in the Rust queue/shared heap and Haxe Int64, but are not universally exact in Wren Num. Use a lossless integer view or equivalent representation before promising round trips for every touch ID.\n\nNested enums and path buffers are covered by plugin_window_events_wren. Zyntax's separate shared-buffer/enum/retained-value gap remains tracked by c5751252b0e8b1e9a019fb93e9db98caf8bc2a9dc5bee7b2f890bfac10344c23.","files":null}]}